+curve paths, local floor offsets, conveyor lanes and water-volume bindings once
+offline. moving stones, moving lotus platforms, water floaters, elevators and
+conveyor-carried boxes use the same support and transport representation.
+prefabs whose moving subtree cannot be identified remain unsupported.
+
+each exported support also retains `endpoints.initial` and `endpoints.terminal`,
+world-axis-aligned boxes enclosing the solid collider vertices at the path's first
+and last poses. these boxes are illustration metadata. navigation continues to
+use the separate support surfaces, transfers and collision checks. closed paths
+can have coincident endpoint boxes. a seesaw's boxes show its authored initial
+board pose and positive tilt limit, without implying a timed terminal state.
+conveyor ownership resolves its numeric level through the scene's level config.
+
+`poses` retains whole-support boxes at the sampled collider poses. `surfacePoses`
+is parallel to the raw support faces and retains every contributing body and
+pose interval when faces coincide. transfers reference their own endpoint poses.
+ordinary moving supports keep a fixed orientation, so boxes interpolate along
+each sampled translation segment. seesaw faces instead reference the discrete
+tilt sample that won their upper-envelope interval. these references let a route
+select boxes at its actual boarding, alighting or support contact. the authored
+first and last boxes remain separate illustration metadata.
+
+selected route exports can include `supportBoxes` at actual boarding, alighting
+or borrowed-support poses. baked support faces retain raw source candidates.
+the annotator resolves selected connection contacts and moving-only walking
+intervals, then selects one feasible pose per conveyor borrow. overlapping
+conveyor instances do not produce multiple boxes for one contact. continuous
+lift samples coalesce into boarding and alighting poses. a route ending on a
+support records a final use pose. legacy catalogues without pose provenance omit
+these optional diagrams. annotation preparation and execution have separate
+`timingMs` fields and are included in command time.
+
+the current fully-enabled model uses the upper envelope of a swept support
+without enumerating motion phase. endpoint caps permit ordinary jumping on and
+off. transport retains authored speed where available, while static collision
+and active exclusions still constrain the complete passenger trajectory.
+different poses of the same device do not become permanent ceilings for each
+other. query preparation checks local clearance and preserves the ordinary
+terrain graph alongside the additions.
+
+the support-query overlay shares the static collision world's body-padded query
+scope. its clearance-baked meshes retain their own build cores. a ground query
+near a core edge can therefore inspect its complete body footprint without
+throwing against a narrower auxiliary index. raw moving-roof hits still require
+a matching clearance-baked support projection. queries beyond the static world's
+scope continue to fail explicitly.
+
+seesaws use the piecewise upper envelope of a board rotating about its local x
+axis. this retains the low pivot and independently raised ends. the resulting
+surface is an optimistic phase-free support abstraction. tilt and waiting time
+are not calibrated and no angular ride duration is invented. phase scheduling,
+boarding cues and measured gameplay timing remain separate follow-up work.
+
+preparation reports `moving-supports`, `timingMs.moving` and per-transfer rejection
+evidence. a changed device catalogue invalidates both moving preparation and
+target refinement, including changes that retain the same number of faces.
+
+## controlled support placement
+
+`traversal-devices` exports forklift supports by their effective controller,
+including the unmarked variant. the moving collider subtree is placed at its
+authored local lower and upper positions. the chassis remains static. both poses
+retain sampled body provenance and timed rides under the fully-enabled snapshot.
+
+`followingSupports` exports movable boxes separately from swept surfaces. Each
+source retains its actual solid collider, restored spawn and authored activity
+radius. Saved endpoints determine the restored spawn rather than a second free
+placement. The planner proposes useful stationary placements in this activity
+range, checks their local support and collision, then clearance-bakes the real
+roof for the ordinary route search. The selected placement is the arrangement
+witness and is drawn as a contact box in the existing route overlay.
+
+Relocation within the activity range is an explicit planning assumption. This
+model does not search activation, escort paths, player separation, phase or
+manipulation duration. It never grants a walkable plane over the complete range
+and never uses two alternative poses of the same source in one layout. Candidate
+coverage is bounded by the existing action-search budget. Unresolved static
+collision ownership is retained and reported instead of deleting geometry by
+bounds. Coordinated multi-box layouts require further candidate coverage.
+
+A data-only export audit found 18 forklifts and 23 following boxes in map01, and
+5 forklifts and 9 following boxes in map02. Existing moving supports and the other
+catalogue fields remain identical. Focused synthetic tests cover the placement
+contracts; real-map route and browser acceptance is a separate step.
